  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nThe core idea is to use a multi-resolution optimization approach with gradient-based refinement to avoid getting stuck in local optima. Unlike previous methods that relied on multiple heuristic phases, we combine global exploration and local refinement in a unified framework. The key innovation is:\n\n- Using a theoretically derived correlation model to guide optimization\n- Implementing efficient line search technique with curvature awareness\n- Adapting the problem by increasing grid resolution to expose better solutions\n- Maintaining explicit gradient tracking for rapid convergence\n\nThe algorithm addresses bottlenecks by:\n   * Explicitly handling constraint satisfaction through tailored projection\n   * Using second-order derivative information for smarter steps\n   * Dynamically adjusting resolutio to explore different regimes\n   * Maintaining a direct link to gradient magnitude for stability\n   * Batching multiple constraints with scale-invariant normalization\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\nVariables:\n- h: Piecewise-constant function represented on equally spaced points [0,2]\n- Common rate \u03bb = 2/n for scaling\n- Gradient components: \u0394h and \u0394v for value/gradient components\n- Lag index k from -(n-1) to (n-1)\n- Four constraint groups for projection\n\nObjective:\nMinimize C\u2085 = (1/\u03bb) * max\u2096 \u2211\u1d62 h[i] * (1-h[i+k])\n\nThis formulation maintains boundary terms exactly while avoiding circular wrap-around. The optimization minimizes this quantity subject to:\n\n0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n\u2211\u1d62 h[i] = n/2\nGradient integrals converge as n \u2192 \u221e\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\nPhase 1: Global exploration (gradient-free)\n   - Initialize grid n=400\n   - Set plateau_size = 80 (perturbation threshold)\n   - Compute corr[h] = \u2219(h\u2297(\u207bh)) \n   - Track gradients \u2207c\u2085 from centered shifts\n   - Use gradient projection with \u03b4 = 0.03\n\nPhase 2: Steepest descent refinement\n   - For each i (100-200 points):\n     a. Compute gradient \u2207\u1d62 = \u2211\u2096 weight[k] \u2219 rot(h[i+k])\n     b. Apply line search with \u03bc_i = 0.8 \u2219 log(history)\n     c. Saturate changes above \u03b1 = 0.05/mag(\u2207)\n     d. Project \u2207 onto constraint manifold\n\nPhase 3: Scale-invariant update\n   - Normalize gradients by max-shift distance\n   - Update rule: h[i] \u2248 h[i] - (\u03bd / ||\u2207||\u00b2) \u2219 \u2207[i]\n\nPhase 4: Resolution increase\n   - If plateau_size < 20 for 50 steps:\n      \u25b9 Double grid (n=800)\n      \u25b9 Use projection interpolation preserving jumps\n      \u25b9 Maintain four leading solutions\n\nPhase 5: Finish-up\n   - Project final h to satisfy sum = n/2\n   - Verify bounds [0,1] for all elements\n   - Return best_candidate, best_score\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations\n- Initial lambda schedule: 0.02-0.005\n- Gradient tracking window: 20 steps decay factor 0.95\n- Zero-lag exclusion: middle 20% for correlation penalty\n- Dynamic plateau adaptation: reset at every 100 steps\n- Cooldown mechanism when backtracking occurs\n- Block corruption prevention via random walk\n\n5. Compute Budget and Timings\n- Lean gradient evaluation: \u223c30ms per score\n- Resolution change: O(1) due to linear interpolation\n- Main loop: 1300 seconds\n   * Exploration: 400 seconds\n   * Refinement: 600 seconds\n   * Progress reporting: 200 seconds\n   * Resource monitoring: 100 seconds\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\n- Project final h to feasible set project_feasible(h, target)\n- Compute c\u2085 via (max corr) * (2.0/n)\n- Verify:\n   * 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n   * |sum(h) - n/2| < 1e-6\n   * c\u2085 is finite float\n\nReturn (h, c\u2085, n)",
